//! Probe: the modulo operator and the if-expression both compile on forgen
//! 1.4.3, and both produce the right value.
//!
//! `docs/DATARA-ERGONOMICS.md` section 6 lists "No `break` / `continue` / `%` /
//! if-expressions" as a constant friction cost. That document was written
//! against 1.3.4 and re-tested against 1.4.0, so the entry is three releases
//! stale rather than wrong about its own time. Two thirds of it are now retired;
//! `break` and `continue` are the third and are covered by the editor's keyword
//! table instead.
//!
//! Why this is a probe and not a note: the claim it retires is one a reader
//! would act on. "This language has no modulo" is a reason not to use it.
//!
//!     forgen build probes/modulo_ifexpr
//!     ./main.exe
//!
//! Expected, and it is what the binary prints:
//!
//!     1    7 % 3
//!     1    5 > 0, so the if-expression takes the first arm
//!
//! A wrong value here would be a silent miscompile of the same class as the
//! field-offset defect, so the numbers are chosen to be distinguishable: `%`
//! returning the operands, a sum, or 0 would all print something else, and an
//! if-expression that always took one arm would print 2 for the second line.

fn main() -> Int {
    mut rem = 7 % 3
    out rem

    mut n = 5
    mut sign = if n > 0 { 1 } else { 2 }
    out sign
    return 0
}
